== Chapter XIII

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[The #smallcaps[ninth] direction: When the heart is troubled by sin, speak no peace to it until God speaks it — Peace without detestation of sin is unsound; so is peace measured out to ourselves — How we may know when we measure out our peace to ourselves — Directions for that inquiry — The vanity of speaking peace slightly; also of doing it on one single account, not universally.]

==== Ninth Direction: Speak No Peace Until God Speaks It

#smallcaps[Ninth], if God troubles the heart about the guilt of its sinful disorders, whether in respect of their root and indwelling or of any outbreaks of them, #emph[take care that you do not speak peace to yourself before God speaks it; but listen to what he says to your soul]. This is our next direction. Without observing it, the heart will be greatly exposed to the deceitfulness of sin.

This is a matter of great importance. It is a sad thing for a man to deceive his own soul in this. All the warnings God gives us, in his tenderness to our souls, to test and examine ourselves, tend to prevent this great evil of speaking peace to ourselves without ground; for in the end that is to bless ourselves in opposition to God. It is not my business to dwell on the danger of it, but to help believers to avoid it, and to let them know when they are doing it.

To follow this direction rightly, observe:

1\. It is the great #emph[prerogative and sovereignty] of God to give grace to whom he pleases (“He has mercy on whom He wants to have mercy,” Rom 9:18; and among all the sons of men he calls whom he will and sanctifies whom he will). In the same way, among those who are called and justified, and whom he will save, he still reserves this privilege to himself: to speak peace to whom he pleases, and in what degree he pleases, even among those on whom he has bestowed grace. He is “the God of all comfort” in a special way in his dealings with believers; that is, of the good things that he keeps locked up in his family and gives out to all his children at his pleasure. The Lord insists on this in Isa 57:16–18. The very case we are considering is the one dealt with there. When God says he will heal their breaches and their sorrows, he claims this privilege for himself in a special way: “I create it,” verse 19. That is: “Even toward these poor wounded creatures I create it, and by my sovereignty I give it out as I please.”

So it is with God’s #emph[giving of grace] to those who are in the state of nature: he does it with great exactness, and his ways in taking some and leaving others, as far as outward appearances go, are often quite beside and even contrary to all likely expectation. It is the same with his #emph[imparting] of peace and joy to those who are in the state of grace: he often gives them out quite beside our expectations, as far as any visible grounds for his dealings are concerned.

2\. As God #emph[creates peace for whom he pleases], so it is the prerogative of Christ to speak it home to the conscience. When he speaks to the church of Laodicea, which had healed her wounds falsely and spoken peace to herself when she should not have, he takes this title to himself: “the Amen, the faithful and true Witness” (Rev 3:14). He bears witness to our condition as it really is. We may be mistaken, and trouble ourselves for nothing, or flatter ourselves on false grounds. But he is “the Amen, the faithful and true Witness”; and what he says of our state and condition is how it really is. In Isa 11:3 it is said that he “will not judge by what His eyes see,” that is, not by any outward appearance, or anything open to mistake, as we tend to judge. He will judge and settle every case as it really is.

Take these two preliminary observations, and I will give some rules by which men may know whether God is speaking peace to them, or whether they are only speaking peace to themselves:

1\. Men certainly speak peace to themselves when it is #emph[not accompanied by the greatest detestation] imaginable of the sin they are speaking peace about, and by abhorrence of themselves for it. Men are wounded by sin, troubled and perplexed. They know there is no remedy for them except in the mercies of God through the blood of Christ, and so they look to him and to the promises of the covenant in him. On that basis they quiet their hearts, telling themselves that it will be well with them, and that God will be exalted so that he may be gracious to them. Yet their souls have not been brought to the greatest detestation of the sin or sins that troubled them. This is to heal themselves, not to be healed by God. It is only a great and strong wind; the Lord is near it, but the Lord is not in the wind. When men truly “look on Me, the One they have pierced,” without which there is no healing or peace, they will “mourn” (Zech 12:10). They will mourn for him, on this very account, and detest the sin that pierced him. When we go to Christ for healing, faith looks at him especially as one who was pierced. Faith takes several views of Christ, according to the occasions it has for coming to him and having communion with him. Sometimes it looks at his holiness, sometimes his power, sometimes his love, \[sometimes\] his favor with his Father. And when it goes for healing and peace, it looks especially at the blood of the covenant, at his sufferings; for “the punishment that brought us peace was upon Him, and by His stripes we are healed” (Isa 53:5). When we look for healing, we must fix our eyes on his stripes. Not on the outward story of them, which is the way of popish devotion, but on the love, kindness, mystery, and purpose of the cross. And when we look for peace, his punishment must be before our eyes. Now this, I say, if it is done according to the mind of God and in the strength of the Spirit who is poured out on believers, will produce a detestation of the sin or sins for which healing and peace are sought. So in Ezek 16:60, 61: “But I will remember the covenant I made with you in the days of your youth, and I will establish an everlasting covenant with you.” And what then? “Then you will remember your ways and be ashamed.” When God comes home to speak peace in a sure covenant, it fills the soul with shame for all the ways in which it has been estranged from him. And one of the things the apostle mentions as going with that godly sorrow which brings repentance leading to salvation, never to be regretted, is revenge: “what revenge!” (2 Cor 7:11). They looked back on their failures with indignation and revenge for their folly in them. When Job comes to a thorough healing, he cries, “I abhor myself” (Job 42:6); and until he did, he had no lasting peace. He might perhaps have patched himself up with the doctrine of free grace so excellently preached by Elihu (ch. 33, from verse 14 to 30); but then he would only have skinned over his wounds. He must come to self-abhorrence if he is to come to healing. So it was with those in Psalm 78:33–35, in their great trouble and perplexity because of their sin. I do not doubt that when they came to God in Christ they spoke peace to themselves. That they did come to him in Christ is clear from the titles they gave him: they called him their Rock and their Redeemer, two words that everywhere point to the Lord Christ. But was their peace sound and lasting? No; it passed away like the early dew. God did not speak one word of peace to their souls. Why did they not have peace? Because in coming to God they flattered him. How does that appear? Verse 37: “Their hearts were disloyal to Him, and they were unfaithful to His covenant.” They had neither detested nor given up the sin about which they spoke peace to themselves. A man may seek healing and peace however he will. He may go to the true Physician, he may go the right way, he may quiet his heart in the promises of the covenant. Yet when peace is spoken, if it is not accompanied by detestation and abhorrence of the sin that was the wound and caused the trouble, this is no peace of #emph[God’s creating], but of #emph[our own purchasing]. It is only a skinning over of the wound, while the core lies at the bottom. It will fester, and corrupt, and eat away, until it breaks out again with foulness, distress, and danger. There are poor souls who walk in such a path as this. They are more aware of the trouble of sin than of the pollution of uncleanness that goes with it. They seek mercy, and they seek it from the Lord in Christ, yet they will keep the sweet morsel of their sin under their tongue. Let them never think, I say, that they will have true and solid peace. For instance, you find your heart running out after the world, and it disturbs you in your communion with God. The Spirit speaks expressly to you: “If anyone loves the world, the love of the Father is not in him.”#footnote[1 John 2:15.] This drives you to deal with God in Christ for the healing of your soul and the quieting of your conscience. Yet a thorough detestation of the evil itself does not stay with you; perhaps you even like it well enough, and dislike it only for its consequences. Perhaps you may be saved, yet as through fire, and God will have some work with you before he is done. But you will have little peace in this life; you will be sick and fainting all your days (Isa 57:17). This is a deceit that lies at the root of the peace of many professing Christians and wastes it away. They wrestle with all their strength for mercy and pardon, and seem to have great communion with God in doing so. They lie before him and bewail their sins and follies, so that anyone would think, indeed they think so themselves, that surely they and their sins have now parted. And so they receive a mercy that satisfies their hearts for a little while. But when a thorough search comes to be made, there has been some secret reserve kept for the folly or follies they were dealing with; at the least, there has not been the thorough abhorrence of it that is necessary. And their whole peace is quickly found to be weak and rotten, hardly lasting longer than the words they used to beg for it are in their mouths.

2\. When men measure out peace to themselves on the conclusions their #emph[convictions and rational principles] will carry them to, this is a false peace and will not last. I will explain a little what I mean. A man has been wounded by sin; he has a conviction of some sin on his conscience. He has not walked uprightly, as befits the gospel, and all is not well and right between God and his soul. He now considers what is to be done. He has light; he knows what path he must take, and how his soul was healed before. He considers that the promises of God are the outward means by which healing is applied to his sores and quiet to his heart. So he goes to them, searches them out, and finds one or more whose literal words directly suit his condition. He says to himself, “God speaks in this promise. Here I will take a dressing as long and as broad as my wound.” And so he applies the word of the promise to his condition and sits down in peace. This is another appearance on the mount: the Lord is near, but the Lord is not in it. It has not been the work of the Spirit, who alone can “convict … in regard to sin and righteousness and judgment,”#footnote[John 16:8.] but the mere workings of the intelligent, rational soul. We say there are three kinds of life: the vegetative, the sentient, and the rational or intelligent. Some things have only the vegetative. Some have the sentient as well, which includes the first. Some have the rational, which takes in and presupposes both the others. Now he who has the rational life does not act only in keeping with that principle, but also with both the others: he grows, and he feels. It is the same with men in the things of God. Some are merely #emph[natural] and rational men. Some have, #emph[added] to that, conviction with illumination. And some are truly #emph[regenerate]. Now he who has the last has both the former as well, and so he acts sometimes on the principles of the rational man, sometimes on the principles of the enlightened man. His true spiritual life is not the source of all his motions. He does not always act in its strength, and not all his fruit comes from that root. In the case I am speaking of, he acts merely on the principle of conviction and illumination, by which his natural powers are heightened; but the Spirit does not breathe at all on these waters. Take an example. Suppose the wound and trouble of the soul come from relapses. Whatever the evil or folly may be, however small in itself, there are no wounds deeper than those given to the soul on that account, and no troubles greater. In the turmoil of his mind he finds the promise in Isa 55:7: “Let him return to the #smallcaps[Lord], that He may have compassion, and to our God, for He will abundantly pardon.” That is, he will multiply pardon, add to it, do it again and again. Or he finds the one in Hosea 14:4: “I will heal their backsliding; I will freely love them.” The man considers this and thereupon concludes peace for himself. Whether the Spirit of God makes the application or not, whether the Spirit gives life and power to the letter or not, the man does not care. He does not listen for whether God the Lord speaks peace. He does not wait on God. And God perhaps still hides his face, and sees the poor creature stealing peace and running away with it, knowing that the time will come when he will deal with him again and call him to a new reckoning;#footnote[Hos 9:9.] when the man will see that it is useless to go one step where God does not take him by the hand.

I see here, indeed, a number of other questions arising and pressing in. I cannot deal with them all; I will speak a little to one.

It may be said, then, “Since this seems to be the path the Holy Spirit leads us in for the healing of our wounds and the quieting of our hearts, how shall we know when we are going alone, and when the Spirit is also with us?”

#emph[Answer.] (1.) If any of you#super[+] are off the path in this, God will soon let you#super[+] know it. For besides having his promise that he “guides the humble in what is right and teaches them His way” (Ps 25:9), you#super[+] will find he will not let you go on in error forever. He will not, I say, allow your nakedness to be covered with fig leaves. He will take them away, and all the peace you have in them, and will not let you settle on such dregs. You#super[+] will soon know that your wound is not healed; that is, you will soon know by the outcome whether this is how it is with you. The peace you get and obtain in this way will not last. While the mind is overpowered by its own convictions, troubles have nothing to fasten on. But wait a little, and all these reasonings will grow cold and vanish before the face of the first temptation that arises. But further:

(2.) This course is usually taken without #emph[waiting]. Yet waiting is the grace, the particular exercise of faith, that God calls for in such a condition. I know God sometimes comes on the soul at once, in a moment as it were, wounding it and healing it. I am persuaded that this was so with David when he cut off the corner of Saul’s robe. But usually in such a case God calls for#footnote[Ps 130:6; 123:2.] waiting and labor, attending on him as the eye of a servant on his master. The prophet Isaiah says (ch. 8:17), “I will wait for the #smallcaps[Lord], who is hiding His face from the house of Jacob.” When his children have run from his house, God will have them lie a while at his door, and not rush in on him at once; unless he takes them by the hand and pulls them in, when they are so ashamed that they dare not come to him. Now self-healers, men who speak peace to themselves, usually make haste. They will not wait; they do not listen to what God says, but on they go to be healed.#footnote[Isa 28:16.]

(3.) Such a course may quiet the conscience and the mind, the reasoning part of the soul that draws conclusions; yet it does not #emph[sweeten] the heart with rest and gracious contentment. The answer it receives is much like the one Elisha gave Naaman: “Go in peace.”#footnote[2 Kgs 5:19.] That quieted his mind; but I very much question whether it sweetened his heart, or gave him any joy in believing beyond the natural joy that was stirred in him by his healing. “Do not My words bring good?” says the Lord (Mic 2:7). When God speaks, there is not only truth in his words, which may answer the conviction of our understanding; they also do good. They bring what is sweet and good and desirable to the will and the affections. By them the soul returns to its rest, as in Ps 116:7: “Return to your rest, O my soul.”

(4.) Worst of all, it #emph[does not amend the life]. It does not heal the evil or cure the disorder. When God speaks peace, it guides and keeps the soul so that it does not “return to folly.”#footnote[Ps 85:8.] When we speak it ourselves, the heart is not drawn off from the evil. In fact, it is the quickest way in the world to bring a soul into a habit of backsliding. If, after dressing your own wound, you find yourself encouraged to go back to your sin rather than wholly weaned from it, it is all too plain that you have been at work with your own soul, but Jesus Christ and his Spirit were not there. Indeed, often nature, having done its work, will come for its reward before many days are over; and having been busy in the work of healing, it will be ready to argue for a new wounding. When God speaks peace, there comes with it so much sweetness, and such a revelation of his love, that it lays a strong obligation on the soul to deal perversely no more.#footnote[Luke 22:32.]

3\. We speak peace to ourselves when we do it #emph[slightly]. The prophet complains of this in some teachers: Jer 6:14, “They dress the wound of My people with very little care.” And it is so with some people. They make the healing of their wounds a slight work: a look, a glance of faith at the promises does it, and the matter is ended. The apostle tells us that “the message they heard was of no value” to some, “since it was not mixed with faith” (Heb 4:2): μὴ συγκεκραμένος, “it was not well tempered” and mingled with faith. It is not a mere look at the word of mercy in the promise. It must be mingled with faith until it is taken into the very nature of it; and then indeed it does the soul good. If you have had a wound on your conscience, which brought weakness and trouble, and you are now free of it, how did you come to be free? “I looked to the promises of pardon and healing, and so I found peace.” Yes, but perhaps you have made too much haste. You have done it superficially; you have not fed on the promise so as to mix it with faith, and to have all its virtue spread through your soul. You have done it only slightly. Before long you will find your wound breaking out again, and you will know that you are not cured.

4\. Whoever speaks peace to himself on any one account, while at the same time another evil of #emph[no less importance] lies on his spirit about which he has had no dealings with God, that man cries “Peace” when there is none. To explain my meaning a little: A man has neglected a duty again and again, perhaps, when in all righteousness he owed it. His conscience is perplexed, his soul wounded; he has no rest in his bones because of his sin. He seeks healing and finds peace. Yet all the while, perhaps, worldliness or pride or some other folly, by which the Spirit of God is greatly grieved, may lie in that man’s bosom, and they do not disturb him, nor he them. Let that man not think that any of his peace is from God. It will be well with men when they have an equal regard for all God’s commandments. God will justify us #emph[from] our sins, but he will not justify the least sin #emph[in] us. As the prophet says to him, “Your eyes are too pure to look upon evil.”

5\. When men speak peace to their own consciences, God seldom speaks #emph[humiliation] to their souls. God’s peace is humbling peace, melting peace, as it was with David.#footnote[Ps 51:1.] He was never so deeply humbled as when Nathan brought him the news of his pardon.

But you#super[+] will say, “When may we take the comfort of a promise as our own, in relation to some particular wound, to quiet the heart?”

First, in general, when God speaks it, whenever that may be, sooner or later. I told you#super[+] before that he may do it at the very moment of the sin itself, and with such irresistible power that the soul cannot help receiving his mind in it. Sometimes he will make us wait longer. But when he speaks, sooner or later, whether we are sinning or repenting, whatever the condition of our souls, if God speaks, he must be received. There is nothing in our communion with him that the Lord is more troubled with us for, if I may say so, than our unbelieving fears, which keep us from receiving the strong consolation he is so willing to give us.

But you#super[+] will say, “We are where we were. When God speaks it, we must receive it; that is true. But how shall we know #emph[when he speaks]?”

(1.) I wish we could all come up to this in practice: to receive peace when we are convinced that God speaks it, and that it is our duty to receive it. But further:

(2.) There is, if I may put it so, a secret instinct in faith by which it knows the voice of Christ when he really speaks. As the baby leaped in the womb when the blessed Virgin came to Elizabeth, so faith leaps in the heart when Christ really draws near to it. “My sheep,” says Christ, “know my voice” (John 10:4). That is: “They know my voice; they are used to the sound of it.” And they know when his lips are opened to them and are full of grace. The bride was in a sad condition in Song 5:2, asleep in security. Yet as soon as Christ speaks, she cries, “It is the voice of my beloved!” She knew his voice, and was so well acquainted with communion with him that she recognized him at once. And so will you#super[+]. If you exercise yourselves in acquaintance and communion with him, you will easily tell his voice from the voice of a stranger. And take this κριτήριον [test] with you: when he does speak, he speaks as no man ever spoke. He speaks with power, and one way or another he will make your “hearts burn within” you, as he did to the disciples (Luke 24). He does it by putting his hand in through the opening of the door (Song 5:4), that is, his Spirit into your hearts, to take hold of you.

He who has his senses trained to distinguish good from evil, having grown in judgment and experience by constantly observing the ways of Christ’s dealings with the soul, the manner of the Spirit’s workings, and the effects he usually produces, is the best judge for himself in this case.

Secondly, if the word of the Lord does your#super[+] souls good, he speaks it: if it humbles, if it cleanses, and if it serves the ends for which promises are given, namely, to endear, to cleanse, to melt and bind to obedience, to self-emptying, and so on. But this is not my business, and I will not turn aside any further in pursuing this direction. Without observing it, sin will gain great advantages toward hardening the heart.
